  "worked_solution": "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case:\n\n- the sample size is \n\n$$\nn=101,\n$$\n\n- the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}f({x}_{i}) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0.1+2\\cdot 0.7+4\\cdot 0.1+8\\cdot 0.1 \\\\  & =0+1.4+0.4+0.8 \\\\  & =2.6,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =({0}^{2}\\cdot 0.1+{2}^{2}\\cdot 0.7+{4}^{2}\\cdot 0.1+{8}^{2}\\cdot 0.1)-{2.6}^{2} \\\\  & =(0+2.8+1.6+6.4)-6.76 \\\\  & =4.04.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(2.6,\\frac{4.04}{101}) \\\\  & ∼N(2.6,0.04).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(2.5<\\overset{―}{X}<3).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2.5<\\overset{―}{X}<3) & ≈P(\\frac{2.5-2.6}{\\sqrt{0.04}}<Z<\\frac{3-2.6}{\\sqrt{0.04}}) \\\\  & =P(-0.5<Z<2) \\\\  & =Φ(2)-Φ(-0.5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(2.5<\\overset{―}{X}<3)=Φ(2)-Φ(-0.5).\n$$",
